mode: "100644"
text: "# The PROVE framework — original research that earns links + AI citations\n\nDesign a study people *have* to cite. Each letter is a gate; skipping one is where studies fail (no data void,\nsoft method, cooked analysis, buried finding, or no distribution).\n\n## P — Pick a question inside a data void\nA claim worth proving where good public data doesn't exist **and** your audience + journalists + AI engines\nwould cite the answer. Your unfair advantages, in order of strength:\n1. **Proprietary/internal data** — your platform, your customers, your transactions. Highest originality; no one\n   else can replicate it. (Anonymize before analysis.)\n2. **A recurring survey of your niche** — become the annual source (\"State of X\"); the cadence compounds.\n3. **Fresh analysis of a public dataset** — lower originality but real and fast; great when you have no list.\nReject questions already answered well, questions you can't get real data for, and questions whose answer no one\nwould repeat. The test: *write the headline stat you hope to find — would a journalist or an AI cite it?*\n\n## R — Run a sound method\nChoose the method that fits the question, then design it **before** collecting:\n- **Define**: population, sample frame (who's eligible + how you reach them), target n, recruitment, time window.\n- **Instrument**: neutral, non-leading questions (not \"don't you agree X is overpriced?\"); one idea per question;\n  answer options that don't bias.\n- **Plan the analysis up front** (which comparisons, which segments) so you're not fishing later.\nMethod ladder: proprietary-data analysis > representative survey > public-dataset analysis > simple experiment.\nThe agent designs the instrument; the **human/tool fields it and gathers the real data**.\n\n## O — Observe honestly (collect + analyse without cooking it)\n- Real data only. **Never invent or AI-synthesize data points** — the fastest way to lose the asset and the brand.\n- No p-hacking (don't hunt until something is \"significant\"); no cherry-picking the flattering cut; only segment\n  where n supports it.\n- **Disclose**: sample size, dates, method, and limitations — prominently, not in fine print. Small n? Frame as\n  *directional*, not definitive; never say \"most people\" off 42 responses.\n- Conflict of interest (a self-funded study about your own category) gets disclosed, not hidden.\n\n## V — Voice the one finding that travels\nFind the **surprising-but-defensible** headline — one number people will repeat — fully supported by the data\nand never inflated (38% is not \"nearly half\"). Shape it: a clear claim (**X% of Y do Z**), the *why it matters*,\nand a memorable name/number. One hero stat carries the study; 2–3 supporting findings back it. Counter-intuitive\nbeats confirmatory — but only if the data truly says it.\n\n## E — Engineer for citation, then distribute\nBuild the asset to be cited and then spread it:\n- **Report page**: visible methodology + sample + date, a **\"Last Updated\" stamp**, clean charts, and a\n  **copy-paste stat box with an attribution link** (so anyone quoting you links back).\n- **Atomize** into cuts → the format writers (carousel/thread/reels/caption) + infographic-and-data-viz.\n- **Distribute wide**: pitch journalists the news angle; seed across multiple publications (distribution is the\n  citation multiplier); refresh on a cadence (beat the freshness cliff).\n- **WoopSocial publishes** the cuts + promo posts and drives traffic to the report; measure citations + saves +\n  referral traffic, never a fabricated metric.\n"
